1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
A cold morning, and you own one garment.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:03,424
It is a hide.

3
00:00:03,424 --> 00:00:04,162
Not a coat.

4
00:00:04,162 --> 00:00:09,799
A hide, folded around the shoulders, held shut by one hand while the other does the work.

5
00:00:09,799 --> 00:00:15,121
That is the picture most of us carry of prehistoric dress, and it is probably wrong.

6
00:00:15,121 --> 00:00:17,555
A single hide is a bad answer to cold.

7
00:00:17,555 --> 00:00:25,954
It hangs open at the neck, stiffens as it dries, and does not grip the wrist or the ankle — exactly where heat leaves a body fastest.

8
00:00:25,954 --> 00:00:30,209
So the interesting question is not how someone wore an animal skin.

9
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:35,594
It is when clothing stopped being something you drape and became something you build.

10
00:00:35,594 --> 00:00:38,656
Today we follow that change through three finds.

11
00:00:38,656 --> 00:00:41,090
Hide-working tools in a Moroccan cave.

12
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:43,900
A needle you could lose between two fingers.

13
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:47,904
And a set of beads that turned a warm garment into a statement.

14
00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:51,092
We do not have one ancient garment from any of it.

15
00:00:51,092 --> 00:00:53,400
We have the workshop, and the trace.

16
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,183
Clothes rot.

17
00:00:54,183 --> 00:00:55,824
That is the whole problem.

18
00:00:55,824 --> 00:00:59,182
Hide is skin: protein, water, and a meal for bacteria.

19
00:00:59,182 --> 00:01:08,305
It disappears long before the rest of a site does — so for most of prehistory we are not looking at clothing, we are looking at the shadow it casts.

20
00:01:08,305 --> 00:01:12,338
And one line of evidence that is not archaeological at all: lice.

21
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:13,857
Head lice live on heads.

22
00:01:13,857 --> 00:01:17,644
Body lice live in clothing, because they need fabric to grip.

23
00:01:17,644 --> 00:01:25,663
The moment those two become separate lineages is roughly the moment humans wore clothes often enough for a parasite to specialise.

24
00:01:25,663 --> 00:01:37,300
A 2011 genetic study by Melissa Toups, Andrew Kitchen and David Reed put that split at no less than eighty-three thousand years ago, and possibly as early as a hundred and seventy thousand.

25
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:39,432
That is a window, not a timestamp.

26
00:01:39,432 --> 00:01:46,225
But it places the habit deep in the African Middle Stone Age, long before people carried it into cold country.

27
00:01:46,225 --> 00:01:48,111
Clothing was not an invention.

28
00:01:48,111 --> 00:01:49,507
It was an inheritance.

29
00:01:49,507 --> 00:01:51,210
Which changes the question.

30
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:55,120
Not, did they wear clothes — but how well could they make them?

31
00:01:55,120 --> 00:02:01,256
Contrebandiers Cave sits on the Atlantic coast of Morocco, close enough to the sea that you can hear it.

32
00:02:01,256 --> 00:02:11,317
From layers dated between a hundred and twenty thousand and ninety thousand years ago, Emily Hallett and her colleagues recovered more than twelve thousand bone fragments.

33
00:02:11,317 --> 00:02:13,471
Sixty-two had been shaped by people.

34
00:02:13,471 --> 00:02:20,486
The tool you would walk past is a scraper: the rib of an antelope or a wild cow, split into a flat, spatula-like shape.

35
00:02:20,486 --> 00:02:21,819
It looks like nothing.

36
00:02:21,819 --> 00:02:30,064
Under a microscope it carries a record — short deep grooves, and a polish from being pulled again and again across the inside of a raw hide.

37
00:02:30,064 --> 00:02:33,506
You do not scrape a hide because you want to eat it later.

38
00:02:33,506 --> 00:02:39,526
Scraping removes fat, membrane and hair so that skin stops being a corpse and starts being a material.

39
00:02:39,526 --> 00:02:42,206
The detail that settles it is on the animals.

40
00:02:42,206 --> 00:02:50,686
Hallett found cut marks across the paws and jaws of sand foxes, golden jackals and wildcats — a pattern that does not match butchering for meat.

41
00:02:50,686 --> 00:02:55,241
The skin had been opened at the feet and pulled, in one piece, over the head.

42
00:02:55,241 --> 00:02:57,511
You do that when the fur is the point.

43
00:02:57,511 --> 00:03:00,133
It does not prove anyone there sewed a coat.

44
00:03:00,133 --> 00:03:02,872
The researchers call it strong proxy evidence.

45
00:03:02,872 --> 00:03:07,720
But it shows an animal chosen for its pelt, and a tool that makes the result soft.

46
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:11,704
If you want clothing from hides, the difficult step is not cutting.

47
00:03:11,704 --> 00:03:12,572
It is joining.

48
00:03:12,572 --> 00:03:15,851
You can fold a hide, or tie it with a strip of leather.

49
00:03:15,851 --> 00:03:25,480
But for a sleeve, or a seam that holds under strain, you have to pass thread through material — with a tool fine enough not to tear the hole wider than the thread.

50
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:29,347
Awls came first: a sharpened bone, pushed through to open a hole.

51
00:03:29,347 --> 00:03:33,507
They work — but they leave a gap, and every puncture weakens the hide.

52
00:03:33,507 --> 00:03:38,491
A needle carries the thread with it, so the hole is exactly the width of the thread.

53
00:03:38,491 --> 00:03:45,474
The oldest bone needle most often cited comes from Denisova Cave in southern Siberia, around fifty thousand years old.

54
00:03:45,474 --> 00:03:51,810
It is about seven centimetres long, made from the bone of a large bird, with an eye worked through the end.

55
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:58,734
It came from layers that also produced Denisovan remains — though it cannot be assigned to a species with confidence.

56
00:03:58,734 --> 00:04:04,835
By fifty thousand years ago, someone was drilling a hole through a sliver of bone without splitting it.

57
00:04:04,835 --> 00:04:06,468
That is not a crude object.

58
00:04:06,468 --> 00:04:07,748
That is a specialist.

59
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:09,145
And then something odd.

60
00:04:09,145 --> 00:04:11,894
Eyed needles do not appear everywhere at once.

61
00:04:11,894 --> 00:04:19,700
They show up in the cold parts of Eurasia from around forty thousand years ago, as the climate tips toward the last glacial maximum.

62
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:21,156
People already had awls.

63
00:04:21,156 --> 00:04:22,789
People already had clothes.

64
00:04:22,789 --> 00:04:24,480
So why invent a harder tool?

65
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:30,220
In 2024, Ian Gilligan and colleagues argued that the answer is not survival in the simple sense.

66
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:32,697
A scraped hide gives you a loose garment.

67
00:04:32,697 --> 00:04:34,285
Enough for a cool evening.

68
00:04:34,285 --> 00:04:44,296
Not enough for a glacial winter, where what keeps you alive is not one thick layer but several thin ones — and the layer closest to the skin is the one that has to fit.

69
00:04:44,296 --> 00:04:45,706
That needs fine sewing.

70
00:04:45,706 --> 00:04:47,709
Fine sewing needs an eyed needle.

71
00:04:47,709 --> 00:04:51,906
In Gilligan's phrasing, the innovation comes down to making underwear.

72
00:04:51,906 --> 00:04:57,647
People decorate themselves when skin is visible: ochre, scars, paint, beads pressed to the body.

73
00:04:57,647 --> 00:05:04,870
Cover the body completely, for months, because staying warm is life and death, and all that signalling has nowhere to go.

74
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:06,695
So it moves onto the clothing.

75
00:05:06,695 --> 00:05:13,858
Eyed needles attach beads and shells and strips of fur to the outside of a garment, fine enough to read from a distance.

76
00:05:13,858 --> 00:05:16,751
Clothing stops being thermal and becomes social.

77
00:05:16,751 --> 00:05:20,889
It is a hypothesis, built from timing and tool, and Gilligan says so.

78
00:05:20,889 --> 00:05:23,960
But once you look for it, the record is full of it.

79
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:30,328
In the foothills of the Caucasus, in the republic of Georgia, there is a cave called Dzudzuana.

80
00:05:30,328 --> 00:05:36,097
In 2009, a team led by Eliso Kvavadze and Ofer Bar-Yosef was not looking for clothing.

81
00:05:36,097 --> 00:05:42,465
They were studying ancient climate, which meant pollen, and counting grains under a microscope.

82
00:05:42,465 --> 00:05:44,910
What they found instead were fibres.

83
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:45,625
Wild flax.

84
00:05:45,625 --> 00:05:50,065
Thousands of them, in layers up to thirty-four thousand years old.

85
00:05:50,065 --> 00:05:53,440
Some were twisted — spun, deliberately, into cord.

86
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,688
Some were knotted.

87
00:05:54,688 --> 00:05:59,393
And some were coloured: black, grey, turquoise, and one that was pink.

88
00:05:59,393 --> 00:06:01,373
The colours came from plants.

89
00:06:01,373 --> 00:06:10,134
Someone had taken a plant, extracted fibre, spun it into thread, and dyed it — for cord, for baskets, for sewing, or for all three.

90
00:06:10,134 --> 00:06:12,512
No textile survives from Dzudzuana.

91
00:06:12,512 --> 00:06:16,819
What survives is fibre, at a scale you need a microscope to see.

92
00:06:16,819 --> 00:06:19,263
Dyeing is not a survival technology.

93
00:06:19,263 --> 00:06:22,971
You do not colour a rope because the rope works better.

94
00:06:22,971 --> 00:06:32,465
Around the same period, at Pavlov in the Czech Republic, fired clay preserved the impression of woven fibre — a textile that no longer exists.

95
00:06:32,465 --> 00:06:36,372
So it is not hides versus cloth, with cloth arriving late.

96
00:06:36,372 --> 00:06:40,080
It is both, for a very long time, in the same toolkits.

97
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:44,201
This is where reconstructions get confident and the evidence does not.

98
00:06:44,201 --> 00:06:47,099
We know the tools, the fibres, the marks on bone.

99
00:06:47,099 --> 00:06:55,180
What we do not have is a complete garment older than about five thousand years — and that one, from a tomb in Egypt, survived by accident.

100
00:06:55,180 --> 00:07:01,572
We do not know whether a hide garment was a rectangle with a hole for the head or a shaped coat with sleeves.

101
00:07:01,572 --> 00:07:10,760
Every image of a prehistoric person in a stitched, fitted outfit is a reconstruction: a reasonable guess built from the tools, not a photograph of an object.

102
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:16,204
Two hundred kilometres east of Moscow, near the town of Vladimir, is a site called Sunghir.

103
00:07:16,204 --> 00:07:21,470
Around thirty-four thousand years ago, people were buried there with extraordinary care.

104
00:07:21,470 --> 00:07:26,617
The best known grave holds a man between roughly thirty-five and forty-five years old.

105
00:07:26,617 --> 00:07:30,044
He was covered in red ochre, and he was covered in beads.

106
00:07:30,044 --> 00:07:31,810
About three thousand of them.

107
00:07:31,810 --> 00:07:38,381
Mammoth ivory, each one carved, each one pierced, each one small enough to be a problem for a modern jeweller.

108
00:07:38,381 --> 00:07:42,520
Alongside them: armbands of ivory, and hundreds of pierced fox teeth.

109
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:50,574
Then those beads were attached to what he was wearing — they were found lying in rows, in the pattern of a garment, as if sewn onto it.

110
00:07:50,574 --> 00:07:53,823
That is the moment clothing stops being about weather.

111
00:07:53,823 --> 00:07:55,174
A hide keeps you warm.

112
00:07:55,174 --> 00:08:04,237
A hide carrying three thousand hand-cut beads announces who this person was, what their community could spend, and what they believed the dead deserved.

113
00:08:04,237 --> 00:08:10,214
Nearby, two children were buried head to head, with ivory spears and discs and thousands more beads.

114
00:08:10,214 --> 00:08:13,760
Whatever the beads meant, they were not a privilege of age.

115
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,809
So: what did ancient humans wear before tailors?

116
00:08:16,809 --> 00:08:18,607
They were already tailoring.

117
00:08:18,607 --> 00:08:25,032
The tailor — a specialist who fits a garment to a body — is present in the tools long before any shop.

118
00:08:25,032 --> 00:08:30,707
What changed across those ninety thousand years was not whether people could make clothes.

119
00:08:30,707 --> 00:08:32,568
It was what clothes were for.

120
00:08:32,568 --> 00:08:33,491
First shelter.

121
00:08:33,491 --> 00:08:34,101
Then fit.

122
00:08:34,101 --> 00:08:37,338
Then, once the body was covered all year, identity.

123
00:08:37,338 --> 00:08:39,074
Almost none of it survives.

124
00:08:39,074 --> 00:08:44,311
The softest, most constant object in a human life is the one the ground eats first.

125
00:08:44,311 --> 00:08:55,175
What we have left is a scraper, a needle with a hole the width of a hair, and a handful of fibres dyed turquoise by someone who had no reason to, except that it looked good.

126
00:08:55,175 --> 00:08:58,974
The next time you pull on a coat, notice what you are doing.

127
00:08:58,974 --> 00:09:05,149
You are putting on the only technology that has to be rebuilt for every single body that wears it.

128
00:09:05,149 --> 00:09:08,385
If you enjoyed this, subscribe to Atlas Understory.

129
00:09:08,385 --> 00:09:13,560
And tell us: given a hide, a bone needle and no thread, what would you make first?
